Raw dev notes -- Making the AI Portal Real: Auth, Users, and UX Polish

Okay, so this past week has been a whirlwind getting the AI agent platform (let's call it the "Mission Control Platform" or MCP for short) ready for prime time. The whole point of this thing is to give users a robust, secure, and intuitive way to interact with our various AI agents – think chat, transcription, and other cool tools. We're moving from a proof-of-concept to something that feels like a proper product, and that means a lot of foundational work.

This period was all about shoring up the core user experience and making it production-ready. A huge chunk of it was around **authentication and user management**. We finally got QMS chat parity, which means durable chat history (thank god, no more losing conversations!), proper authentication, and monitoring in place. This was a big one. On top of that, I built out full user management for admins, including an approval email flow for new signups. Self-service password reset was also a must-have, so that's in now too, complete with email tokens. We also had to strip out some old branding from the portal and set up a dedicated signup path, which was a bit of a cleanup job.

Beyond auth, there was a lot of **email infrastructure and branding**. I set up a two-mailbox Graph sender (one for system notifications, one for agent-specific comms) and then went through the tedious but necessary process of branding various operational emails with a shared shell – logos, signatures, the works. This included branding the transcribe notifications and adding feedback links.

**Privacy** was another key focus. We implemented redaction of prompt and response content from our LiteLLM logging, which is super important for user trust. And speaking of user trust, I built out a concrete user-feedback form with an email-on-submit workflow, so we can actually hear from people using the platform.

On the **UI/UX front**, there were a bunch of quality-of-life improvements. The "My Jobs" button got decoupled from the Transcribe tab and now opens a full page, which is a much better experience. We fixed a small but annoying bug where typing dots would disappear during prefill in chat, and added our proper favicon to the Tools UI. Also added a recipient-email override for the transcribe tab, which is a nice touch for flexibility.

One interesting experiment was the **XLSX honesty skill-card**. The idea was to inject a card into chat when an XLSX file was uploaded, reminding the AI to be honest about partial summaries. Turns out, after an A/B baseline eval, our current model (Gemma) already grounds pretty well on clean summaries, so the card was actually redundant. Good to know, saved us from adding unnecessary clutter!

What was hard? Honestly, the sheer volume of small, interconnected tasks. Getting all the email flows right, making sure the branding was consistent, and tying together auth, user management, and the portal signup path felt like untangling a giant ball of yarn. The JWT-TTL correction mentioned in a todo was a subtle one that could have caused headaches down the line. And the XLSX card experiment, while not "hard" in terms of coding, was a good reminder that sometimes your clever ideas aren't actually needed, and it's important to validate assumptions. It felt good to close that loop quickly.

Personally, this sprint felt like a big step towards maturity for the platform. It's less about flashy new AI features and more about building a solid, trustworthy foundation. It's the kind of work that often goes unnoticed but is absolutely critical for adoption and long-term success. It's a grind, but seeing all these pieces come together, making the platform more robust and user-friendly, is really satisfying. It's a lesson in the importance of the "boring" stuff – security, privacy, good UX – that makes a product truly usable.

Next up: Gitea runner tasks and AI-server SSH access (once I get those creds!).
